      "writtenOn": "2025-06-11T13:12:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Instead of creating `unique_ptr`s and storing those, could we just shove all of the `IntergerRangeInfo` objects into a `Vector` and then store the vector index into these maps? Seems a bit simpler then all of the unique pointers?",
      "revId": "fecee09252c6aca777a19dd747d20ab019d7bb2e",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b2508f0c_95f1194e",
        "filename": "src/tint/lang/core/ir/analysis/integer_range_analysis.cc",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 1118,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002241
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-11T13:44:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Would still need to store them as pointers in that case because pushing to the `Vector` could trigger a reallocation, and we want to be able to return a pointer that is valid for the lifetime of the `IntegerRangeAnalysisImpl`.\n\nAnother option would be to just return the `IntegerRangeInfo` objects by value instead of by pointer. It has a `std::monostate` option that we could use to signal \"no valid range\". The `sizeof(IntegerRangeInfo)` is only ~2x the size of a pointer anyway, so it\u0027s not too much extra overhead for the returns. I could go either way. This CL seems fine to me as is.",
      "parentUuid": "0adb863c_9cafa8fc",
      "revId": "fecee09252c6aca777a19dd747d20ab019d7bb2e",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
